WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.369 --> 00:00:04.000
Changes in your diet not
only affect you physically,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.370
physiologically inside,

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:09.450
but also mentally,
how well you think,

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.220
psychologically,
how well you feel.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.370
But you’ll never know just
how good you can feel

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:19.390
until you put it to the test
and try eating healthy.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.300
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.680
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.150
Today we feature a
wide variety of research

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.900
and we start with a study
that reveals an effective way

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.050
to help people reduce
their meat consumption.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.390
According to a survey of more
than 30,000 U.S. residents,

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:42.050
a third of American adults
self-identify as meat reducers,

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:46.020
meaning one in three of us is trying
to cut down on our meat consumption.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.260
Why?

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.359
For those earning less than
$40,000 a year, the #1 reason is cost;

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:55.620
for those earning more than $40K,
the #1 reason is health.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.870
And indeed, if we were
to define a healthy diet,

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.760
compared to how we're eating now,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.079
we should be eating more
plant-based foods,

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:05.830
including fresh fruits and vegetables,
whole grains, legumes --

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:08.860
meaning beans, split peas,
chickpeas, and lentils -- seeds and nuts,

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.700
and at the same time
lower in animal foods,

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.240
particularly fatty and
processed meats.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.760
In an editorial entitled

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.409
"Plant-Based Diets for Personal,
Population, and Planetary Health,"

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:23.060
co-authored by the chair
of nutrition at Harvard,

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.280
healthy plant-based diets are
not only more sustainable,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.329
but have also been associated
with lower risk of chronic diseases

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:31.710
such as obesity, type 2 diabetes,
cardiovascular disease,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.890
and some cancers.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.439
What do we mean by plant-based?

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:38.659
Basically, any diet that reduces
the amount of animal products

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:40.479
and increases the amount of plants --

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:44.360
again, vegetables, fruits, whole grains,
legumes, nuts, and seeds.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.570
Transitioning global diets towards
healthy plant-based dietary patterns

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.960
would require large-scale
public health efforts

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:55.310
but could be instrumental in ensuring
future human and planetary health.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:59.320
Indeed, the IPCC, the Intergovernmental
Panel on Climate Change,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.369
describes plant-based diets as
a major opportunity for mitigating

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:04.570
and adapting
to climate change

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.170
and includes a policy recommendation
to reduce meat consumption.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.619
Okay, but how do you do it?

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:13.700
In a systematic review of
experimental studies on strategies

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.349
to reduce meat consumption,

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:19.080
one of the most effective experiments
came out of the Midwest.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.900
Sadly, research shows that the
provision of information on its own

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:28.350
can be of limited utility in
facilitating behavioral change.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.920
However, default interventions have
been successfully employed

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.960
in a variety of prosocial contexts,
making the right option or

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.599
the healthiest option,
the easiest option --

00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:40.790
the default option.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.100
Take for example,
organ donation.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.129
Every year, thousands
of people in the United States

00:02:46.129 --> 00:02:50.709
have died waiting for
a suitable donor organ.

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:54.250
But wait -- 85% of Americans
approve of organ donation,

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:58.150
yet less than half have made
a decision about donating, and

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.269
fewer still have granted permission
by signing a donor card.

00:03:02.269 --> 00:03:05.000
If you look at Europe, there's
nearly a ten-fold difference

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.590
in the organ donor rates
across different countries.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.450
In some countries, consent
is only about 10%,

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.700
while in others
it's up to like 99.9%.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.910
What's the difference
between the two colors?

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.491
Gold is opt-in;
blue is opt-out.

00:03:16.491 --> 00:03:19.980
In opt-out countries, the default
is that people are organ donors

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.080
unless they actively
register not to be.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.940
In the opt-in countries
like the United States,

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.049
the default is nobody
is an organ donor

00:03:27.049 --> 00:03:30.599
without explicitly registering
to be one.

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:33.750
So there's all sorts of calls for
campaigns to change public attitudes

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.860
about organ donation, but remember
85% are already on board.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.370
If we want to change behavior
and not just attitudes,

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.150
changing the default condition
may be more effective.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.599
So does it work for diet?

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:52.390
In the DEFAULT treatment,
participants received at their table

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.420
a menu listing only
five meat-free options.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.500
But they were informed -- verbally
and in writing on the menu --

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.019
that they could also consult a second
menu that was posted on the wall

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:04.909
about a dozen feet away,
which had your standard array

00:04:04.909 --> 00:04:07.860
of popular nonvegetarian
dining hall dishes.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.000
And in the control condition
both lists of options

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.340
were mixed together on the
same menu they were handed.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:15.142
And when you do that,
only a minority of people

00:04:15.142 --> 00:04:17.880
choose the meat-free options,
between 5 and 40%,

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.870
depending on if you describe
the meat-free options

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:23.699
in an appealing way, like
pasta with Provencal vegetables,

00:04:23.699 --> 00:04:26.889
or in unappealing terms
like vegan calzone.

00:04:26.889 --> 00:04:29.979
Okay, but what about
the default condition,

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:32.509
where the menu in front
of them is all meat-free.

00:04:32.509 --> 00:04:35.470
They can still get up and order
all the meat they want,

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.260
but the alternate menu
is a few steps away.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:41.510
You're not taking away
any people's options,

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.479
but just by making it the default,

00:04:44.479 --> 00:04:48.690
meat-free choices shot up
like to 75 and 90%.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:53.530
Even an unappealingly described
meat-free option totally won out.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.340
And even just adding more veg
options, from a quarter of the options

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.759
to half the options,
may increase the sales

00:04:59.759 --> 00:05:04.880
of the veg options
between about 40 to 80%.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:05.990
In our next story,

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:08.570
I share a touching story of the
power of plant-based eating

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.479
for chronic kidney failure.

00:05:11.479 --> 00:05:14.170
Is it possible to ameliorate
chronic kidney disease

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:15.881
using a whole food,
plant-based diet?

00:05:15.881 --> 00:05:16.881
In my last video
on kidney disease,

00:05:16.881 --> 00:05:17.881
I talked about how randomizing
people to cut just around

00:05:17.881 --> 00:05:18.881
10 grams of protein from their
daily diet could cut their risk

00:05:18.881 --> 00:05:19.881
of dialysis and death
by a whopping 77%.

00:05:19.881 --> 00:05:20.881
That was cutting protein
across the board.

00:05:20.881 --> 00:05:21.881
But while animal-based
protein ingestion —

00:05:21.881 --> 00:05:22.881
meat, dairy, and egg white
protein ingestion —

00:05:22.881 --> 00:05:24.889
promotes an acidic environment
in the kidneys, inflammation, and

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:28.190
stresses the kidneys to what's
called hyperfiltration mode,

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:31.010
plant-based protein can be
alkaline-producing and

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.229
anti-inflammatory and contain
kidney-protective properties.

00:05:34.229 --> 00:05:38.910
So, what if you have kidney patients
eat a plant-dominant low-protein diet,

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:44.110
abbreviated adorably as PLADO,
I guess for plant-dominant.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.160
If you fashion up a plant-based diet
index score where you get points

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.260
for healthy plant foods

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.250
and get points deducted
for eating animal foods,

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.340
those with serious kidney disease
with higher scores were found

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:57.670
to have lower systemic inflammation.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:01.320
But does that actually translate
into living a longer life?

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:02.420
Apparently so.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:05.870
Even a 10% increase in the
proportion of plant-based protein

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.740
was associated with a significant
reduction in all-cause mortality.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:13.090
Even just eating more servings of
fruits and vegetables, like two a day

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:17.960
compared to two a week,
is linked to living longer.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.150
Without fully functioning kidneys,

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.290
there are concerns about
phosphorus and potassium overload,

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:25.330
though, on a plant-based diet.

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:28.990
But the phosphorus in plant-based
foods is not as much of a problem

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:32.620
as the phosphorus additives
in processed and animal foods.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.500
And the risk of potassium overload
from plant-based diets appears

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.740
overstated and not supported
by the evidence.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:42.199
But you don't know about
ameliorating chronic kidney disease

00:06:42.199 --> 00:06:47.500
using a whole food, plant-based
diet until you put it to the test.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.500
Here's a case report of a 69-year-old
man with type 2 diabetes,

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:54.560
high blood pressure, and
stage 3 chronic kidney disease,

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.700
resulting in elevated phosphorus
and potassium in the blood,

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:02.780
Interested in changing his diet
to improve his medical condition.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.630
That's my kind of patient!

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:08.509
He was on 12 different medications,
eating a diet that may actually be

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:11.040
slightly better than
the average American:

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.000
some whole grains and beans,

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.700
but then his doctor advised to try
eating whole food, plant-based.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:20.260
So, oatmeal with fruit and flax,
beans and greens,

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:23.530
whole wheat spaghetti and
veggies, fruit as snacks.

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:26.590
Counselled to eat as much as he
wanted from whole healthy foods;

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:30.419
no carb counting, no calorie counting,
no portion size restriction —

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:35.290
improving the quality of food rather
than restricting the quantity of food.

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:38.080
He adopted the whole food,
plant-based diet, packed with carbs,

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.759
yet rapidly reduced his insulin
requirements by more than 50%,

00:07:42.759 --> 00:07:45.190
and subsequently saw
improvements in weight,

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:47.129
blood pressure,
and cholesterol.

00:07:47.129 --> 00:07:49.400
Because eating healthy
can have such a rapid effect

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:52.370
on improving your body's
insulin sensitivity,

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:54.940
immediate adjustments
in insulin dosing were made.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.819
Within four days, his insulin dose
was able to be reduced

00:07:57.819 --> 00:08:02.169
from roughly 210 units of insulin
a day down to 70 units daily,

00:08:02.169 --> 00:08:05.110
and an oral blood-sugar lowering
medication had to be stopped

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:08.220
due to rapidly improving blood sugar.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:12.190
He also was able to stop his
carvedilol, the hydrochlorothiazide,

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:15.080
amlodipine, sitagliptin
within the first two months

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:17.500
due to improving blood pressure
and blood sugars.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:20.370
His insulin dose was
steadily titrated downward.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:25.030
His pravastatin dose was cut in half,
and he lost about 50 pounds.

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:29.139
Okay, so what happened
to his stage 3 kidney failure?

00:08:29.139 --> 00:08:31.930
He was no longer in
stage 3 kidney failure!

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:32.930
Doctors watching this will understand
what all these numbers mean.

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:33.930
Here's a graph of his GFR, which
is a measure of kidney function,

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:34.930
declining for years before shooting
up after he started eating healthy.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:37.110
He experienced an increase
in estimated GFR of 73%,

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:38.950
suggesting that the improvement
in estimated kidney function

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:42.229
was greater than what would be
expected from weight loss alone.

00:08:42.229 --> 00:08:45.260
For example, lose about 60 pounds
from bariatric surgery,

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:48.800
and you only get
about a 12 to 15% boost.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:51.900
Bottom line: for individuals
with chronic kidney disease,

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.390
especially those with obesity,
hypertension, or diabetes,

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:58.260
a strict, all-you-care-to-eat
whole food, plant-based diet

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.460
may confer significant benefit.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.830
I mean, apart from the
kidney-specific outcomes,

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:07.730
overall mortality is significantly
lower among kidney patients

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:09.300
who eat more plants.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:12.560
And that's critical because most
kidney patients don't even make it

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:16.010
to dialysis because they die first,

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:19.280
most often from
cardiovascular disease.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.650
Let's hear from the patient.

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:25.089
"At the outset, it seemed like
this was going to be a difficult

00:09:25.089 --> 00:09:26.339
and restrictive way to eat,

00:09:26.339 --> 00:09:29.709
but I began feeling different
almost immediately

00:09:29.709 --> 00:09:32.720
and we had to decrease
my insulin after ONE day.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:37.320
It seemed like almost overnight I had
more energy than I'd had in years.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.750
Weight I'd been trying to lose for
a decade began dropping off.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:44.890
As the weight came off, I felt lighter,
and more able to move my body again.

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:49.930
This lifestyle change has been
the greatest gift I've ever received.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:53.519
I am off most of my medications,
I've lost over 70 pounds,

00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:57.390
and I've regained control
over my health.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:59.329
I feel empowered
by this lifestyle change,

00:09:59.329 --> 00:10:02.780
and I finally feel like I'm
in charge of my health,

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:07.510
not just an unlucky victim shuffling
from one specialist to the next.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:13.557
My only regret was that
I didn't know about this sooner."

00:10:13.557 --> 00:10:14.315
Finally today,

00:10:14.315 --> 00:10:16.500
we look at how stress
and menstrual cycles

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.950
can affect the smell of our breath.

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:23.100
According to the American
Dental Association,

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:28.000
about 50% of American adults
suffer from oral malodor,

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.959
with prevalence rates around the world
ranging from 2% to nearly 80%.

00:10:32.959 --> 00:10:37.410
On average, it seems to be about
one in three of us on the planet Earth

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:39.170
have bad breath.

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:42.660
What effect might
stress have on the smell of our breath?

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.150
Stressed students were found
to have significantly higher levels

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:47.810
of the "rotten egg" gas,
hydrogen sulfide,

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:52.269
which is one of the main volatile sulfur
compounds related to bad breath,

00:10:52.269 --> 00:10:56.140
originating from the degradation of the
sulfur-containing amino acid cysteine,

00:10:56.140 --> 00:11:00.279
found concentrated in animal
proteins like meat and dairy.

00:11:00.279 --> 00:11:04.529
Were they eating different diets
or just too busy to brush?

00:11:04.529 --> 00:11:08.389
The simplest explanation
is just the dry mouth you get

00:11:08.389 --> 00:11:11.290
when you're super-stressed,
part of our fight or flight response.

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:13.660
It's the same reason
we get morning breath,

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:16.730
because we have decreased
saliva production when we sleep

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:18.529
that would otherwise
self-clean the mouth,

00:11:18.529 --> 00:11:21.519
keeping it from becoming
like a stagnant pond.

00:11:21.519 --> 00:11:25.320
Though maybe stress hormones
are having an effect as well.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:29.320
We suspect sex hormones may play
a role, since though men and women

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.139
have the same before-and-after
rise in bad breath compounds

00:11:32.139 --> 00:11:34.430
after a stressful situation,

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:37.790
women seem to start out
with higher baseline levels.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.730
Gender appears to play
an important role.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:43.870
Women have significantly worse
morning breath, for example,

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:45.990
and bad breath is affected
by the menstrual cycle.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:49.480
In fact, that's listed as one of the
causes: so-called menstrual breath.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:52.360
As you can see, there are higher
levels of bad breath compounds

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:55.780
in the mouths of women in the
premenstrual and menstrual phases

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:57.600
compared not just to men,

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.800
but the follicular phase
of their own cycle,

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:01.709
meaning like the first half
before ovulation.

00:12:01.709 --> 00:12:07.610
Hmm, so maybe bad breath is a hormonal
thing more than just a dry mouth thing.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:11.769
But salivary flow is also lower in
menstrual and premenstrual phases.

00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:12.769
Check it out: significantly
less salivary flow

00:12:12.769 --> 00:12:13.769
during menstrual flow
and right before.

00:12:13.769 --> 00:12:15.930
So, is this all just about having
a drier mouth during stress

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:17.690
and certain times of the month?

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:20.320
How could you tease out the effects?

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:23.920
Well, what about studying
stressful periods.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:25.779
PMS, premenstrual syndrome,

00:12:25.779 --> 00:12:29.880
is a stressful state characterized
by irritability, tension, mood swings.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:34.959
Is the menstrual dry mouth and
bad breath just due to period stress?

00:12:34.959 --> 00:12:35.959
Apparently so.

00:12:35.959 --> 00:12:38.449
If you split women up
into those who experience PMS and

00:12:38.449 --> 00:12:41.709
those who don't, it's only those
with PMS who suffer the rise

00:12:41.709 --> 00:12:44.399
in bad breath compounds
as their period arrives.

00:12:44.399 --> 00:12:48.480
But the salivary flow was
not statistically different.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.209
So the results suggest that
a stressful situation can be

00:12:51.209 --> 00:12:54.370
a predisposing factor for bad breath
that may have nothing to do

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:56.949
with dry mouth or salivary flow.

00:12:56.949 --> 00:12:59.269
So what's going on?

00:12:59.269 --> 00:13:02.760
It's the effects of the stress
hormones themselves

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.209
on the production of
bad breath compounds.

00:13:05.209 --> 00:13:08.380
They dripped some stress
hormones on bad breath bacteria---

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:10.459
hormones like adrenaline
and cortisol---

00:13:10.459 --> 00:13:14.160
and they started churning out
more hydrogen sulfide.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:16.060
What can we do about it

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:19.000
if we can't treat the cause
and reduce the stress?

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.829
I have videos on dietary
changes that can help

00:13:21.829 --> 00:13:23.839
as well as tongue cleaning methods.

00:13:23.839 --> 00:13:26.560
And I have videos in the works
on the effects of gum chewing

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:28.080
and the best mouthwash to use

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:32.269
that doesn't kill the good
bacteria in your mouth.

00:13:32.269 --> 00:13:34.459
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:13:34.459 --> 00:13:37.550
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:38.870
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:43.680
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:49.380
